From: notmuch2 kernel-optimized
Subject: [PATCH] mm/vmalloc: drop orphaned EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(map_kernel_range)

arter97's tree carries an EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL for map_kernel_range(), but that
function no longer exists anywhere in the tree — it was superseded by
vmap_pages_range(). `grep -rn map_kernel_range` matches only this one export
line; there is no definition and no declaration in include/linux/vmalloc.h.

This is invisible in arter97's own builds because he sets
CONFIG_LAZY_INITCALL=y, which force-disables CONFIG_MODULES (init/Kconfig:
`menuconfig MODULES depends on !LAZY_INITCALL`). With modules off the
EXPORT_SYMBOL* macros expand to nothing, so the dangling reference is never
compiled.

The optimized config restores CONFIG_MODULES=y — required, because with
modules disabled the 328 `=m` symbols in his defconfig (including
CONFIG_QCOM_KGSL, the Adreno GPU driver) build to nothing at all. That makes
the macro expand for real and the build fails with:

  mm/vmalloc.c:639:19: error: use of undeclared identifier 'map_kernel_range'

Removing the stale export is safe: it exported a symbol that does not exist,
so nothing can have been linking against it.

--- a/mm/vmalloc.c
+++ b/mm/vmalloc.c
@@ -636,7 +636,6 @@
 	flush_cache_vmap(addr, end);
 	return err;
 }
-EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(map_kernel_range);

 int is_vmalloc_or_module_addr(const void *x)
 {
